        { "$comment": "P69 (lane cs): `__declspec(align(N))` — the align verb under cl's rule, which differs from GNU `aligned` in exactly the four keys below; every cell MEASURED on cl 19.51 (mingw-w64 gcc ignores the modifier with \"'align' attribute directive ignored\", so cl is the only reference that gives it a meaning). `withoutOperand: ignored` — a bare `__declspec(align)` asks for nothing and is warned: cl refuses it (C2059) and mingw-w64 gcc ignores the modifier whatever its operand, so no reference gives it GNU's bare-`aligned` meaning and this compiler does not invent one. `onTypeAlias: raises` — on a typedef it only ever RAISES: `typedef __declspec(align(1)) int t;` stays 4 and a lower request on an alias of a 32-aligned struct stays 32, where GNU `aligned(2)` on an `int` alias makes a 2-aligned type. `repeated: last` — two of them on one declaration: the LAST one wins and the earlier is warned (16 then 32 gives 32, 32 then 16 gives 16; cl's C4141 \"used more than once\"), where GNU takes the largest. `leadingDecoratesDefinitionOf` — written BEFORE a declaration whose type specifier DEFINES a struct or a union, it aligns THAT TYPE (`__declspec(align(32)) struct S { int a; } s;` — the type 32, its size 32, a later `struct S` object 32; with no declarator at all it is how cl's own documentation aligns a type), and not an enumeration defined there (the enum type stays 4, the declared object gets 32), nor a tag nested inside the body, nor a tag that is only referred to (then only the declared object). Beside `_Alignas` the larger wins, in every order. A raising one on a MEMBER moves the member and raises the struct. `appliesTo` leaves `function` out: cl refuses it there (C2024) and mingw-w64 gcc ignores it, so it is ignored and warned by the kind gate, never applied.", "names": ["__declspec(align)"], "appliesTo": ["variable", "type"], "withinDeclarator": "type", "effect": "align", "withoutOperand": "ignored", "repeated": "last", "leadingDecoratesDefinitionOf": ["structSpec", "unionSpec"] },
